---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { heroDefinition } from '../content/snippets';
---

<Site title="EvalKit — author agent evals as code" description="A local-first kit for writing agent evals as code. Version definitions with your team, reuse fixtures and predicate judges, compare model settings, and inspect saved results." active="home">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <div class="eyebrow"><span class="pulse"></span> EVALS AS CODE, RESULTS AS FILES <span class="eyebrow-line"></span> OPEN SOURCE</div>
      <h1>Write evals <em>as code</em></h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">EvalKit is a kit for authoring agent evals in TypeScript. Version them with your team, reuse fixtures and code-based judges, test across model settings, and keep the results as files you can inspect.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/">Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit">View source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-note"><span class="note-line"></span> Your evals live in your repo. Your runs live on disk.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-code">
      <div class="code-card"><div class="code-header"><span class="file-icon">◇</span> greeting.eval.ts <span class="code-header-right">TYPESCRIPT</span></div><Code code={heroDefinition} lang="ts" theme="github-light" /><div class="code-footer"><span class="footer-check">✓</span> Typed definitions. Reusable parts. Reviewable changes.</div></div>
      <a class="hero-code-source" href="https://github.com/leostera/evalkit/blob/main/examples/starter/evals/greeting.eval.ts">See the runnable starter source <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="value-strip" aria-label="EvalKit at a glance"><div class="wrap value-grid"><div><span class="value-index">01 /</span><strong>Author</strong><span>TypeScript evals you can version.</span></div><div><span class="value-index">02 /</span><strong>Reuse</strong><span>Share fixtures and scoring code.</span></div><div><span class="value-index">03 /</span><strong>Compare</strong><span>Model settings, trials, and results.</span></div></div></section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="section-intro"><span class="section-kicker">THE KIT <span>— 001</span></span><h2>A test suite your team can <span class="text-accent">build together.</span></h2><p>Keep eval definitions in Git, review changes like any other code, and reuse the building blocks instead of rebuilding each test from scratch.</p></div>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature"><div class="feature-icon lime" aria-hidden="true">◇</div><span class="feature-number">01 — WRITE THE TEST</span><h3>Author evals as code.</h3><p>Define tasks, transcripts, trial policies, and success criteria in TypeScript. Diff and review eval changes alongside your app.</p><span class="feature-tag">EVALS + GIT</span></article>
      <article class="feature"><div class="feature-icon lavender" aria-hidden="true">▤</div><span class="feature-number">02 — SHARE THE PARTS</span><h3>Reuse fixtures and judges.</h3><p>Use the same input files and predicate scorers across evals. Keep evaluator-only answers separate from what the agent can see.</p><span class="feature-tag">FIXTURES + SCORING</span></article>
      <article class="feature"><div class="feature-icon peach" aria-hidden="true">⌁</div><span class="feature-number">03 — CHANGE ONE THING</span><h3>Compare model settings.</h3><p>Configure a lazy parameter matrix to run the same eval across settings. Have your adapter apply the selected model.</p><span class="feature-tag">MATRICES + TRIALS</span></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap artifacts-section"><div class="section-intro"><span class="section-kicker">THE OUTPUT <span>— 002</span></span><h2>Version the definition. <span class="text-accent">Keep the result.</span></h2><p>Git tracks the eval code your team reviews. Each local run saves manifests, scores, trajectories, and candidate artifacts as files you can inspect, compare, or selectively archive for a history of results.</p></div><div class="artifact-grid"><div class="artifact-card"><span class="artifact-glyph">↳</span><div><strong>trajectory.jsonl</strong><span>Every recorded event, in order.</span></div></div><div class="artifact-card"><span class="artifact-glyph">◈</span><div><strong>scoring.json</strong><span>Values, explanations, and evidence.</span></div></div><div class="artifact-card"><span class="artifact-glyph">▥</span><div><strong>artifacts/candidate/</strong><span>The files the agent worked with.</span></div></div><div class="artifact-card"><span class="artifact-glyph">▣</span><div><strong>Local dashboard</strong><span>Browse runs and trial evidence.</span></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="cta-band"><div class="wrap cta-content"><div><span class="section-kicker">READY TO TRY IT?</span><h2>Write the eval once.<br /><em>Test the next change.</em></h2><p>Start locally with a runnable example. No hosted account required.</p></div><a class="button button-dark" href="/docs/">Open the getting started guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
</Site>
